  "poe": {
    "budgetW": 2150,
    "sourceUrl": "https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/ecs-48s-poe",
    "note": "Tech specs print PoE availability as four rows against the (2) 1,200 W hot-swappable modules: 200-240 V AC shared 2,150 W / 100-120 V AC shared 1,750 W / 200-240 V AC redundant 950 W / 100-120 V AC redundant 750 W. 2,150 W is the ceiling - one PSU, or a 120 V feed, or a redundant pair drops it to 1,750 / 950 / 750 W respectively, and the lab must state which. Per-port ceiling is 90 W on all 48 copper ports (802.3bt Type 4, Class 8): 15.4 W PoE / 30 W PoE+ / 60 W PoE++ / 90 W PoE+++."
  },
  "notes": "Ethernet census read from the vendor's own Port Layout table: https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/ecs-48s-poe (HTTP 200, 676,185 B, server-rendered spec JSON, read 2026-11-20) prints six rows - \"1 GbE RJ45 = 1 (1G/100M/10M)\" with the note \"OOB Management port only\", \"2.5 GbE RJ45 = 32 (All PoE+++)\", \"10 GbE RJ45 = 16 (All PoE+++)\", \"25G SFP28 = 4\", \"100G QSFP28 = 2\" (note: \"Stacking port only, does not support uplink\"), \"Console = 1\". Before this correction the row carried five of those and 55 jacks for a 56-jack 1U switch - the serial console was absent, so nothing could be cabled to the box's out-of-band path. eth0 is the OOB management port the vendor's note names; it and the 32 x 2.5 GbE ports also now declare the 10M rung the page prints.\n\nPoE: 48 PoE+++ ports (32 x 2.5 GbE + 16 x 10 GbE) with no budget in the spec, so the loader named it from the model string, found no wattage and served 0 W while the description beside it claimed 2,150 W. The four-row availability table is now in `poe.budgetW` + `poe.note`.\n\nAlso from the same page, recorded as prose and not modelled as ports: Max. Power Consumption 250 W excluding PoE / 2,400 W including, Switching Capacity 680 Gbps, Forwarding Rate 506 Mpps, (2) AC input hot-swappable 1,200 W modules."
}
